#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708901 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int_C (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$, where $C$ is the line segment from $(2,1)$ to $(0,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar line-integral formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFor $\\mathbf a=(2,1)$ and $\\mathbf b=(0,1)$, parameterize the segment by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\mathbf a+t(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)=(2-2t,1),\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n$$\n\nThis gives the stated starting point at $t=0$ and ending point at $t=1$. Thus $x=2-2t$ and $y=1$. The derivative and speed are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=(-2,0),\\qquad \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=\\sqrt{(-2)^2+0^2}=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the function gives $f(\\mathbf r(t))=(2-2t)+1=3-2t$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=2\\int_0^1(3-2t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=2[3t-t^2]_0^1\\\\\n&=2(3-1)=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe speed is positive even though $x$ decreases. Reversing the direction does not change an integral with respect to arc length."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708921 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(y+1)\\,\\mathbf i+(x+2y)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\non $\\mathbb R^2$. The diagram shows three piecewise-smooth directed paths: $\\alpha$ goes from $A$ to $B$, $\\beta$ from $B$ to $C$, and $\\gamma$ from $A$ to $C$.\n\n![Directed paths alpha from A to B, beta from B to C, and gamma from A to C.](images/6e/6e8f8843f36ab175203206494f36a05c0c24fc4998a6c3fb0e4e0556ccc1e959.png)\n\nFor any directed path $\\eta$, write $W_\\eta=\\int_\\eta\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$. You are given $W_\\alpha=1$ and $W_\\beta=2$.\n\nWhich statements are true? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First establish that the field is conservative. With $P=y+1$ and $Q=x+2y$,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nBoth components have continuous partial derivatives on the simply connected domain $\\mathbb R^2$. Thus $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, and every closed-path circulation is zero.\n\nFollowing $\\alpha$, then $\\beta$, then the reverse of $\\gamma$ returns to $A$. Reversing a path changes the sign of its work. Therefore,\n\n$$\nW_\\alpha+W_\\beta-W_\\gamma=0,\n\\qquad W_\\gamma=1+2=3.\n$$\n\nEquivalently, the route through $B$ and the direct route have the same endpoints, so path independence gives the same work along them.\n\n- The equation $W_\\alpha+W_\\beta-W_\\gamma=0$ is true: it expresses the circulation around that closed route.\n- The equation $W_\\gamma=0$ is false: $W_\\gamma=3$, not zero. The path $\\gamma$ is open.\n- The equation $W_\\alpha+W_\\beta+W_\\gamma=0$ is false: $W_\\alpha+W_\\beta+W_\\gamma=1+2+3=6$. The plus sign on $W_\\gamma$ does not represent the return path.\n- The zero-circulation statement is true for every piecewise-smooth closed path, regardless of its shape or orientation.\n\nSelect $W_\\alpha+W_\\beta-W_\\gamma=0$ and the statement that every closed path has zero circulation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708926 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(y^2+x)\\,\\mathbf i+(2xy+1)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\non $\\mathbb R^2$. Which statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. All paths below are piecewise smooth and lie in $\\mathbb R^2$." :question/worked-solution "Write $P=y^2+x$ and $Q=2xy+1$. Their cross partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2y=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nThe components have continuous partial derivatives everywhere on the simply connected domain $\\mathbb R^2$, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n\n- The claim that $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative is false: $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n- The claim that every path has zero work is false. For example, on the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(1,0)$, we have $y=0$ and $\\mathrm dy=0$, so the work is $\\int_0^1 x\\,\\mathrm dx=\\tfrac12$, not zero.\n- The claim about ellipses is true. Every ellipse is a closed curve, and the circulation of a conservative field around any closed curve is zero. Reversing the orientation still gives zero.\n\nSelect only the statement that every ellipse has zero circulation in either orientation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708931 :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=x^2+y^2$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ and let $\\mathbf F=\\nabla f$. Which statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. All paths below are piecewise smooth and lie in $\\mathbb R^2$." :question/worked-solution "The field $\\mathbf F=\\nabla f$ is conservative by definition. The fundamental theorem of line integrals gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=f(B)-f(A),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are the starting and ending points.\n\n- The claim that every path has zero work is false. An open path need not have zero work: from $(0,0)$ to $(1,0)$, the work is $1-0=1$.\n- The claim that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative is true because $f$ is a potential function for $\\mathbf F$.\n- The claim that every closed path has zero circulation is true. A closed path has the same starting and ending point, so its circulation is $f(A)-f(A)=0$, in either orientation.\n\nSelect “$\\mathbf F$ is conservative” and the statement that every closed path has zero circulation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708938 :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e4a78829ce8d1135d46dda1db59c968c0306937d9c5a45b24fa41648a679ea4.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(2 x)\\mathbf i+(- x + 2 y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=4,\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n." :question/worked-solution "Use additivity over the two oriented pieces.\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nThe contribution from $C_1$ is supplied, so calculate $C_2$ only. Choose $x=2 - t$ and $y=t$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$ in that order. The endpoint substitutions give the start and end of the stated piece.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_2(t)&=(2 - t)\\mathbf i+(t)\\mathbf j, \\\\ \\mathbf r_2'(t)&=(-1)\\mathbf i+(1)\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the field components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mathbf r_2(t))&=4 - 2 t, \\\\ Q(\\mathbf r_2(t))&=3 t - 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nForm the dot product, keeping both tangent factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r_2(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r_2'(t)&=(4 - 2 t)(-1) \\\\ &\\quad+(3 t - 2)(1) \\\\ &=5 t - 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate in the specified direction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_{0}^{1}(5 t - 6)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{5 t^{2}}{2} - 6 t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the supplied contribution:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=(4)+\\left(- \\frac{7}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708940 :question/problem "![](images/60/6087da076e65bda5240c428ff67c570ad59ae9a8e5a5a3c9e8406184998de56a.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(1,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,-2).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(2 x - 2 y)\\mathbf i+(- x)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=1,\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n." :question/worked-solution "Use additivity over the two oriented pieces.\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nThe contribution from $C_1$ is supplied, so calculate $C_2$ only. Choose $x=1 - t$ and $y=- 2 t$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$ in that order. The endpoint substitutions give the start and end of the stated piece.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_2(t)&=(1 - t)\\mathbf i+(- 2 t)\\mathbf j, \\\\ \\mathbf r_2'(t)&=(-1)\\mathbf i+(-2)\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the field components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mathbf r_2(t))&=2 t + 2, \\\\ Q(\\mathbf r_2(t))&=t - 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nForm the dot product, keeping both tangent factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r_2(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r_2'(t)&=(2 t + 2)(-1) \\\\ &\\quad+(t - 1)(-2) \\\\ &=- 4 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate in the specified direction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_{0}^{1}(- 4 t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[- 2 t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the supplied contribution:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=(1)+(-2)=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708956 :question/problem "![](images/04/0457cb819e6f39ef9c0978e356cff788e1cf5edbef9cbf844a054ab777b70eac.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $B(0,1),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of the line segment from the point $B(0,1)$ to the point $D(1,1).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x + 2 y)\\mathbf i+(- y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\frac{5}{2},\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n." :question/worked-solution "Use additivity over the two oriented pieces.\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nThe contribution from $C_2$ is supplied, so calculate $C_1$ only. Choose $x=2 t - 2$ and $y=t$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$ in that order. The endpoint substitutions give the start and end of the stated piece.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_1(t)&=(2 t - 2)\\mathbf i+(t)\\mathbf j, \\\\ \\mathbf r_1'(t)&=(2)\\mathbf i+(1)\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the field components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mathbf r_1(t))&=4 t - 2, \\\\ Q(\\mathbf r_1(t))&=- t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nForm the dot product, keeping both tangent factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r_1(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r_1'(t)&=(4 t - 2)(2) \\\\ &\\quad+(- t)(1) \\\\ &=7 t - 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate in the specified direction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_{0}^{1}(7 t - 4)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{7 t^{2}}{2} - 4 t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=- \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the supplied contribution:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708960 :question/problem "![](images/73/73c6a8268a179f72611fb65e099d1249d48501e4fd4731d03747800e1944fd97.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,2)$ to the point $D(1,3).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(0)\\mathbf i+(- 2 x + y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n." :question/worked-solution "Use additivity over the two oriented pieces.\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nThe contribution from $C_2$ is supplied, so calculate $C_1$ only. Choose $x=t$ and $y=t + 1$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$ in that order. The endpoint substitutions give the start and end of the stated piece.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_1(t)&=(t)\\mathbf i+(t + 1)\\mathbf j, \\\\ \\mathbf r_1'(t)&=(1)\\mathbf i+(1)\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the field components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mathbf r_1(t))&=0, \\\\ Q(\\mathbf r_1(t))&=1 - t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nForm the dot product, keeping both tangent factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r_1(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r_1'(t)&=(0)(1) \\\\ &\\quad+(1 - t)(1) \\\\ &=1 - t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate in the specified direction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_{0}^{1}(1 - t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[- \\frac{t^{2}}{2} + t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the supplied contribution:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708961 :question/problem "![](images/93/93c6184f39b28a057866bb02e11e5e81a7abea4ff8a9a907670dd30cfc93d557.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(1,-1)$ to the point $B(1,1),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,1)$ to the point $O(0,0).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(y)\\mathbf i+(- x - 2 y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-2,\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n." :question/worked-solution "Use additivity over the two oriented pieces.\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nThe contribution from $C_1$ is supplied, so calculate $C_2$ only. Choose $x=1 - t$ and $y=1 - t$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$ in that order. The endpoint substitutions give the start and end of the stated piece.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_2(t)&=(1 - t)\\mathbf i+(1 - t)\\mathbf j, \\\\ \\mathbf r_2'(t)&=(-1)\\mathbf i+(-1)\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the field components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mathbf r_2(t))&=1 - t, \\\\ Q(\\mathbf r_2(t))&=3 t - 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nForm the dot product, keeping both tangent factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r_2(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r_2'(t)&=(1 - t)(-1) \\\\ &\\quad+(3 t - 3)(-1) \\\\ &=2 - 2 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate in the specified direction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_{0}^{1}(2 - 2 t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[- t^{2} + 2 t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the supplied contribution:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=(-2)+(1)=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708976 :question/problem "![](images/c0/c0a4f042748ddadd824bed4ee71bca9724d7ca7dcc376a0ccf7c1353d59ec4fc.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,2)$ to the point $B(-1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(-1,2)$ to the point $D(-1,0).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- x + y)\\mathbf i+(- x - y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0,\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n.\n\nFor $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r_1(t)=(t - 2)\\mathbf i+(2)\\mathbf j$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$." :question/worked-solution "Use additivity over the two oriented pieces.\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nThe contribution from $C_2$ is supplied, so calculate $C_1$ only. Choose $x=t - 2$ and $y=2$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$ in that order. The endpoint substitutions give the start and end of the stated piece.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_1(t)&=(t - 2)\\mathbf i+(2)\\mathbf j, \\\\ \\mathbf r_1'(t)&=(1)\\mathbf i+(0)\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the field components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mathbf r_1(t))&=4 - t, \\\\ Q(\\mathbf r_1(t))&=- t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nForm the dot product, keeping both tangent factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r_1(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r_1'(t)&=(4 - t)(1) \\\\ &\\quad+(- t)(0) \\\\ &=4 - t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate in the specified direction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_{0}^{1}(4 - t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[- \\frac{t^{2}}{2} + 4 t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the supplied contribution:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)+(0)=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708980 :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e4a78829ce8d1135d46dda1db59c968c0306937d9c5a45b24fa41648a679ea4.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- 2 x - y)\\mathbf i+(- y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=3,\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n.\n\nFor $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r_1(t)=(2 t)\\mathbf i+(0)\\mathbf j$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$." :question/worked-solution "Use additivity over the two oriented pieces.\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nThe contribution from $C_2$ is supplied, so calculate $C_1$ only. Choose $x=2 t$ and $y=0$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$ in that order. The endpoint substitutions give the start and end of the stated piece.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_1(t)&=(2 t)\\mathbf i+(0)\\mathbf j, \\\\ \\mathbf r_1'(t)&=(2)\\mathbf i+(0)\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the field components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mathbf r_1(t))&=- 4 t, \\\\ Q(\\mathbf r_1(t))&=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nForm the dot product, keeping both tangent factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r_1(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r_1'(t)&=(- 4 t)(2) \\\\ &\\quad+(0)(0) \\\\ &=- 8 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate in the specified direction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_{0}^{1}(- 8 t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[- 4 t^{2}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the supplied contribution:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=(-4)+(3)=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708993 :question/problem "![](images/73/73c6a8268a179f72611fb65e099d1249d48501e4fd4731d03747800e1944fd97.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,2)$ to the point $D(1,3).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- 2 x)\\mathbf i+(x + y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=1,\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n.\n\nFor $C_2$, use $\\mathbf r_2(t)=(1)\\mathbf i+(t + 2)\\mathbf j$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$." :question/worked-solution "Use additivity over the two oriented pieces.\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nThe contribution from $C_1$ is supplied, so calculate $C_2$ only. Choose $x=1$ and $y=t + 2$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$ in that order. The endpoint substitutions give the start and end of the stated piece.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_2(t)&=(1)\\mathbf i+(t + 2)\\mathbf j, \\\\ \\mathbf r_2'(t)&=(0)\\mathbf i+(1)\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the field components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mathbf r_2(t))&=-2, \\\\ Q(\\mathbf r_2(t))&=t + 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nForm the dot product, keeping both tangent factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r_2(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r_2'(t)&=(-2)(0) \\\\ &\\quad+(t + 3)(1) \\\\ &=t + 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate in the specified direction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_{0}^{1}(t + 3)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{t^{2}}{2} + 3 t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the supplied contribution:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=(1)+\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708998 :question/problem "![](images/c0/c0a4f042748ddadd824bed4ee71bca9724d7ca7dcc376a0ccf7c1353d59ec4fc.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,2)$ to the point $B(-1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(-1,2)$ to the point $D(-1,0).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- x - y)\\mathbf i+(2 x)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=- \\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n.\n\nFor $C_2$, use $\\mathbf r_2(t)=(-1)\\mathbf i+(2 - 2 t)\\mathbf j$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$." :question/worked-solution "Use additivity over the two oriented pieces.\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nThe contribution from $C_1$ is supplied, so calculate $C_2$ only. Choose $x=-1$ and $y=2 - 2 t$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$ in that order. The endpoint substitutions give the start and end of the stated piece.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_2(t)&=(-1)\\mathbf i+(2 - 2 t)\\mathbf j, \\\\ \\mathbf r_2'(t)&=(0)\\mathbf i+(-2)\\mathbf j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the field components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mathbf r_2(t))&=2 t - 1, \\\\ Q(\\mathbf r_2(t))&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nForm the dot product, keeping both tangent factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r_2(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r_2'(t)&=(2 t - 1)(0) \\\\ &\\quad+(-2)(-2) \\\\ &=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate in the specified direction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_{0}^{1}(4)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ &=\\left[4 t\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the supplied contribution:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)+(4)=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709002 :question/problem "![Segment C from A(-1, 2) to B(1, 1).](images/05/05889a75dfabf783019fb7f4bfc0283999874ade7bd58e564fa6fd153226680d.png)\n\nThe labeled points are the endpoints of the straight segment $C$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Which definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C (x^2-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s?\n$$\n\nDo not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a scalar line integral with respect to arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nRead $A=(-1,2)$ and $B=(1,1)$ from the figure. The displacement is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a=(1-(-1))\\mathbf i+(1-2)\\mathbf j=2\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(t)&=\\mathbf a+t(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)\\\\\n&=(-1+2t)\\mathbf i+(2-t)\\mathbf j,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis starts at $A$ and ends at $B$. Compute the derivative, speed, and substituted function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=2\\mathbf i-\\mathbf j,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{2^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt5,\\\\\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=(-1+2t)^2-(2-t)\\\\\n&=4t^2-3t-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\int_C(x^2-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\sqrt5\\int_0^1(4t^2-3t-1)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nKeep both the initial coordinates and the signs of the coordinate changes when substituting."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709005 :question/problem "Which definite integral is equivalent to the line integral of $f(x,y)=x+\\sin y$ along the line segment from $(2,0)$ to $(2,2)$? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a scalar line integral with respect to arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe endpoint position vectors are $\\mathbf a=2\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf b=2\\mathbf i+2\\mathbf j$. Parameterize the segment by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r(t)&=\\mathbf a+t(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)\\\\\n&=2\\mathbf i+2t\\mathbf j,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$ and $t=1$ we obtain the stated endpoints. Along the segment, $x=2$ and $y=2t$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=2\\mathbf j,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{0^2+2^2}=2,\\\\\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=2+\\sin(2t).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitution gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C (x+\\sin y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=2\\int_0^1 (2+\\sin(2t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe factor $2$ comes from arc length, not from the function substitution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709021 :question/problem "![Segment C from A(1, 3) to B(3, 1).](images/fe/fe2d9715d2f7e62e684d1a4326c7ebe1a3d052e161bdb3de061cdf5fb6640fed.png)\n\nLet $C$ be the straight segment from $A$ to $B$ shown above, and let $f(x,y)=x^2-y$. Use the parameterization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u)=(3-u)\\mathbf i+(1+u)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nWhich definite integral, with increasing limits and parameter $u$, is equivalent to $\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$? Determine the parameter interval from the endpoints. Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "The figure gives $A=(1,3)$ and $B=(3,1)$. Locate these points in the supplied parameterization:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(0)=3\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j=\\mathbf b,\\qquad\n\\mathbf r(2)=\\mathbf i+3\\mathbf j=\\mathbf a.\n$$\n\nThus $0\\le u\\le2$ traces the same segment once, from $B$ to $A$. A scalar line integral with respect to arc length does not change when the traversal is reversed. We may therefore use these increasing limits for $C$.\n\nDifferentiate and substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(u)&=-\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(u)\\|&=\\sqrt{(-1)^2+1^2}=\\sqrt2,\\\\\nf(\\mathbf r(u))&=(3-u)^2-(1+u)\\\\\n&=u^2-7u+8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe arc-length factor is the positive speed, even though $x$ decreases. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_0^2 f(\\mathbf r(u))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(u)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\\\\n&=\\sqrt2\\int_0^2(u^2-7u+8)\\,\\mathrm{d}u.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe usual interval $[0,1]$ belongs to the endpoint parameterization $\\mathbf a+t(\\mathbf b-\\mathbf a)$; it cannot be attached unchanged to this different parameterization."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709116 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a418ee1077c0ff02e482de48f0bd2b6ea9d9349afd2383982268e671d721d4ac.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse and vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Let $C$ traverse the curve once in the **counterclockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$\n\nII. $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$\n\nIII. $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r = 0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$ measures the work done around a *closed* curve. Assistance gives positive work, opposition gives negative work, and perpendicular force gives zero work.\n\nThe field opposes the motion all the way around $C$ in the **counterclockwise** direction.\n\n![](images/ff/ff9321c7e89f068bdea219704e49308395c70d38ed5632d4825dd53d891aa7f7.png)\n\nThus, $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$. Reversing traversal reverses the work: $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$. The reversed path $-C$ is **clockwise**.\n\n![](images/e3/e39aa75ea70dbcbac17d4a5c3a1e2a36e5f3675878c514ee3591d2ce8f127d8f.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$.\n\nI. $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$ is **false**.\n\nII. $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$ is **true**.\n\nIII. $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r = 0$ is **false**.\n\nThe true statements are II only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709118 :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9ac1a76885add140473a1eca1fda60470a75e0ea5fb4ffad5a02ff23e48507f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse and vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Let $C$ traverse the curve once in the **counterclockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$\n\nII. $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$\n\nIII. $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$ measures the work done around a *closed* curve. Assistance gives positive work, opposition gives negative work, and perpendicular force gives zero work.\n\nThe field assists the motion all the way around $C$ in the **counterclockwise** direction.\n\n![](images/a1/a1c8005c52130303d0cbc4a12fc1f3a40a3db87ab36cd287f50283ab80bf3832.png)\n\nThus, $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$. Reversing traversal reverses the work: $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$. The reversed path $-C$ is **clockwise**.\n\n![](images/21/21bf5177e22c35424fe2e1c4f3790d7c6a783b8fdc3f02c2c11c90068b56f36f.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$.\n\nI. $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$ is **false**.\n\nII. $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$ is **true**.\n\nIII. $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$ is **true**.\n\nThe true statements are II and III only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709129 :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9ac1a76885add140473a1eca1fda60470a75e0ea5fb4ffad5a02ff23e48507f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse and vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Let $C$ traverse the curve once in the **counterclockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$ measures the work done around a *closed* curve. Assistance gives positive work, opposition gives negative work, and perpendicular force gives zero work.\n\nThe field assists the motion all the way around $C$ in the **counterclockwise** direction.\n\n![](images/a1/a1c8005c52130303d0cbc4a12fc1f3a40a3db87ab36cd287f50283ab80bf3832.png)\n\nThus, $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$. Reversing traversal reverses the work: $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$. The reversed path $-C$ is **clockwise**.\n\n![](images/21/21bf5177e22c35424fe2e1c4f3790d7c6a783b8fdc3f02c2c11c90068b56f36f.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$.\n\n- $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r = 0$ is **false**.\n\n- $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$ is **true**.\n\n- $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r = 0$ is **false**.\n\nTherefore, select $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709151 :question/problem "![](images/a8/a8aa0a39cf918f69cbd1075113eb051f4a5aa4f67fe531386f09f95ea336ba22.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse and vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Let $C$ traverse the curve once in the **counterclockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$ measures the work done around a *closed* curve. Assistance gives positive work, opposition gives negative work, and perpendicular force gives zero work.\n\nThe field opposes the motion all the way around $C$ in the **counterclockwise** direction.\n\n![](images/b9/b92a2640f547afc8866524c6af86754b7f7851027e2d8e8a5f0e905866cbe5b8.png)\n\nThus, $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$. Reversing traversal reverses the work: $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$. The reversed path $-C$ is **clockwise**.\n\n![](images/2a/2a790a4afed6db8cc1a476b3793a64ba10ea11f94d18e7764189eaa6f694aec0.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$.\n\n- $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$ is **true**.\n\n- $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r = 0$ is **false**.\n\n- $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$ is **true**.\n\nTherefore, select $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$ and $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709152 :question/problem "![](images/21/218bf292c3016879d6e53002f610ba020d8df64bdbfa14d9f1b2b303f8b18e67.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse and vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Let $C$ traverse the curve once in the **counterclockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$ measures the work done around a *closed* curve. Assistance gives positive work, opposition gives negative work, and perpendicular force gives zero work.\n\nThe field assists the motion all the way around $C$ in the **counterclockwise** direction.\n\n![](images/8e/8e1e410cfb38556ebefa6d344f954e3256052a35cf3ad05a4270fe3d34806024.png)\n\nThus, $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$. Reversing traversal reverses the work: $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$. The reversed path $-C$ is **clockwise**.\n\n![](images/15/1590415f4323f7b56283f9718f04a433c235a5e6461eb8239f3dc745f7651923.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$.\n\n- $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$ is **true**.\n\n- $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$ is **false**.\n\n- $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$ is **false**.\n\nTherefore, select $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709164 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a418ee1077c0ff02e482de48f0bd2b6ea9d9349afd2383982268e671d721d4ac.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse and vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Let $C$ traverse the curve once in the **counterclockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$\n\nII. $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$\n\nIII. $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$ measures the work done around a *closed* curve. Assistance gives positive work, opposition gives negative work, and perpendicular force gives zero work.\n\nThe field opposes the motion all the way around $C$ in the **counterclockwise** direction.\n\n![](images/ff/ff9321c7e89f068bdea219704e49308395c70d38ed5632d4825dd53d891aa7f7.png)\n\nThus, $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$. Reversing traversal reverses the work: $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$. The reversed path $-C$ is **clockwise**.\n\n![](images/e3/e39aa75ea70dbcbac17d4a5c3a1e2a36e5f3675878c514ee3591d2ce8f127d8f.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$.\n\nI. $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$ is **true**.\n\nII. $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$ is **true**.\n\nIII. $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$ is **false**.\n\nThe true statements are I and II only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709175 :question/problem "![](images/d2/d232b32fc243beced89ed9ebce168352fe42e008d162c6d55bfbacaac829a2e6.png)\n\nConsider the circle and vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown above. Let $C$ traverse the curve once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$ measures the work done around a *closed* curve. Assistance gives positive work, opposition gives negative work, and perpendicular force gives zero work.\n\nThe field assists the motion all the way around $C$ in the **clockwise** direction.\n\n![](images/2b/2bc25d16cc39cfe026239e2872a28636580cf71194a77d75d31714f63400fa59.png)\n\nThus, $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$. Reversing traversal reverses the work: $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$. The reversed path $-C$ is **counterclockwise**.\n\n![](images/6e/6efae7bba15ad82db3b87f77c7dcc17d67fa3e37bf41b2a365db87f10852c3df.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$.\n\n- $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$ is **false**.\n\n- $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$ is **true**.\n\n- $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$ is **true**.\n\nTherefore, select $\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r < 0$ and $\\oint_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r > 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709183 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(y\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(- x\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nalong the ellipse $C$ with equation\n\n$$\n16 x^{2} + 25 y^{2}=400\n$$\n\ntraversed once clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Write the curve in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{25}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=1.\n$$\n\nChoose the clockwise parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left(5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$, the path starts at $(5,0)$ and moves downward, confirming the orientation.\n\nCirculation is total work around the closed path:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the path into the field and differentiate the path:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(- 5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\\\\n\\mathbf r\\prime(t)&=\\left(- 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(- 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\\\\n&=\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(- 5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=20 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\\n&\\quad+20 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$ and collecting terms gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)=20.\n$$\n\nIntegrate over the complete traversal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Gamma&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(20\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[20 t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\\n&=40 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe dot product is constant on this path."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709185 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(2 x\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nalong the ellipse $C$ with equation\n\n$$\n3 x^{2} + 25 y^{2}=75\n$$\n\ntraversed once clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Write the curve in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{25}+\\frac{y^2}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nChoose the clockwise parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left(5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$, the path starts at $(5,0)$ and moves downward, confirming the orientation.\n\nCirculation is total work around the closed path:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the path into the field and differentiate the path:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\left(2 \\sqrt{3} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(10 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\\\\n\\mathbf r\\prime(t)&=\\left(- 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(- \\sqrt{3} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\\\\n&=\\left(2 \\sqrt{3} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(10 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- \\sqrt{3} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 10 \\sqrt{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\\n&\\quad-10 \\sqrt{3} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$ and collecting terms gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)=- 10 \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nIntegrate over the complete traversal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Gamma&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(- 10 \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[- 10 \\sqrt{3} t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\\n&=- 20 \\sqrt{3} \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe dot product is constant on this path."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709190 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(2 x\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(2 y\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nalong the ellipse $C$ with equation\n\n$$\n25 x^{2} + 3 y^{2}=75\n$$\n\ntraversed once clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Write the curve in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{3}+\\frac{y^2}{25}=1.\n$$\n\nChoose the clockwise parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left(\\sqrt{3} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(- 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$, the path starts at $(\\sqrt{3},0)$ and moves downward, confirming the orientation.\n\nCirculation is total work around the closed path:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the path into the field and differentiate the path:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\left(2 \\sqrt{3} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(- 10 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\\\\n\\mathbf r\\prime(t)&=\\left(- \\sqrt{3} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(- 5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\\\\n&=\\left(2 \\sqrt{3} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- \\sqrt{3} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(- 10 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=44 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$ and collecting terms gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)=44 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\sin^2t=2\\sin t\\cos t$, an antiderivative of $\\sin t\\cos t$ is $\\frac12\\sin^2t$.\n\n\n\nIntegrate over the complete traversal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Gamma&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(44 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[22 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sine-squared term is zero at both endpoints. The varying term therefore integrates to zero; it is not zero at every point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709196 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(y\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(- x\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nalong the circle $C$ with equation\n\n$$\nx^{2} + y^{2}=25\n$$\n\ntraversed once counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "Write the curve in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{25}+\\frac{y^2}{25}=1.\n$$\n\nChoose the counterclockwise parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left(5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$, the path starts at $(5,0)$ and moves upward, confirming the orientation.\n\nCirculation is total work around the closed path:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the path into the field and differentiate the path:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\left(5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(- 5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\\\\n\\mathbf r\\prime(t)&=\\left(- 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\\\\n&=\\left(5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 5 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(- 5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 25 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\\n&\\quad-25 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$ and collecting terms gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)=-25.\n$$\n\nIntegrate over the complete traversal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Gamma&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(-25\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[- 25 t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\\n&=- 50 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe dot product is constant on this path."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709200 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(x\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(y\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nalong the circle $C$ with equation\n\n$$\nx^{2} + y^{2}=4\n$$\n\ntraversed once clockwise." :question/worked-solution "Write the curve in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nChoose the clockwise parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$, the path starts at $(2,0)$ and moves downward, confirming the orientation.\n\nCirculation is total work around the closed path:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the path into the field and differentiate the path:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\\\\n\\mathbf r\\prime(t)&=\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\\\\n&=\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$ and collecting terms gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrate over the complete traversal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Gamma&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(0\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[0\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe dot product is constant on this path."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709222 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- x - 2 y\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(2 x + 2 y\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nalong the circle $C$ with equation\n\n$$\nx^{2} + y^{2}=1\n$$\n\ntraversed once clockwise. Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\Gamma=$ {{value}}" :question/worked-solution "Write the curve in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{1}+\\frac{y^2}{1}=1.\n$$\n\nChoose the clockwise parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$, the path starts at $(1,0)$ and moves downward, confirming the orientation.\n\nCirculation is total work around the closed path:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the path into the field and differentiate the path:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\\\\n\\mathbf r\\prime(t)&=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(- \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\\\\n&=\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\\n&\\quad-2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\\n&\\quad+3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$ and collecting terms gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\sin^2t=2\\sin t\\cos t$, an antiderivative of $\\sin t\\cos t$ is $\\frac12\\sin^2t$.\n\n\n\nIntegrate over the complete traversal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Gamma&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[- 2 t + \\frac{3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\\n&=- 4 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sine-squared term is zero at both endpoints. The varying term therefore integrates to zero; it is not zero at every point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709224 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- x + 2 y\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(- 2 x - y\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nalong the ellipse $C$ with equation\n\n$$\n5 x^{2} + 4 y^{2}=20\n$$\n\ntraversed once counterclockwise. Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\Gamma=$ {{value}}" :question/worked-solution "Write the curve in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{y^2}{5}=1.\n$$\n\nChoose the counterclockwise parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(\\sqrt{5} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$, the path starts at $(2,0)$ and moves upward, confirming the orientation.\n\nCirculation is total work around the closed path:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the path into the field and differentiate the path:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\left(2 \\sqrt{5} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(- \\sqrt{5} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\\\\n\\mathbf r\\prime(t)&=\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(\\sqrt{5} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\\\\n&=\\left(2 \\sqrt{5} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(- \\sqrt{5} \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{5} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=- 4 \\sqrt{5} \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\\n&\\quad-4 \\sqrt{5} \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\\n&\\quad-\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$ and collecting terms gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\sin^2t=2\\sin t\\cos t$, an antiderivative of $\\sin t\\cos t$ is $\\frac12\\sin^2t$.\n\n\n\nIntegrate over the complete traversal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Gamma&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 4 \\sqrt{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[- 4 \\sqrt{5} t - \\frac{\\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\\n&=- 8 \\sqrt{5} \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sine-squared term is zero at both endpoints. The varying term therefore integrates to zero; it is not zero at every point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709225 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- x - 2 y\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(2 x + y\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nalong the circle $C$ with equation\n\n$$\nx^{2} + y^{2}=4\n$$\n\ntraversed once counterclockwise. Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\Gamma=$ {{value}}" :question/worked-solution "Write the curve in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{y^2}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nChoose the counterclockwise parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$, the path starts at $(2,0)$ and moves upward, confirming the orientation.\n\nCirculation is total work around the closed path:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the path into the field and differentiate the path:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\\\\n\\mathbf r\\prime(t)&=\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\\\\n&=\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=8 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\\n&\\quad+8 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\\\\n&\\quad+8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$ and collecting terms gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)=8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 8.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\sin^2t=2\\sin t\\cos t$, an antiderivative of $\\sin t\\cos t$ is $\\frac12\\sin^2t$.\n\n\n\nIntegrate over the complete traversal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Gamma&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 8\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[8 t + 4 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\\n&=16 \\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sine-squared term is zero at both endpoints. The varying term therefore integrates to zero; it is not zero at every point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709226 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(y\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nalong the ellipse $C$ with equation\n\n$$\n9 x^{2} + 4 y^{2}=36\n$$\n\ntraversed once counterclockwise. Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\Gamma=$ {{value}}" :question/worked-solution "Write the curve in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{4}+\\frac{y^2}{9}=1.\n$$\n\nChoose the counterclockwise parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$, the path starts at $(2,0)$ and moves upward, confirming the orientation.\n\nCirculation is total work around the closed path:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma=\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the path into the field and differentiate the path:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\left(- 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j,\\\\\n\\mathbf r\\prime(t)&=\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake the dot product of corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)\\\\\n&=\\left(- 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&\\quad+\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\\\\n&=17 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$ and collecting terms gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime(t)=17 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\sin^2t=2\\sin t\\cos t$, an antiderivative of $\\sin t\\cos t$ is $\\frac12\\sin^2t$.\n\n\n\nIntegrate over the complete traversal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Gamma&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(17 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{17 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sine-squared term is zero at both endpoints. The varying term therefore integrates to zero; it is not zero at every point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709254 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(x + 3 y^{2}\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(6 x y - 1\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\non $\\mathbb R^2$. Which statements are true? Select all that apply. All paths mentioned are piecewise smooth and lie in $\\mathbb R^2$." :question/worked-solution "Write $P=x + 3 y^{2}$ and $Q=6 x y - 1$. The cross partials are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=6 y\\\\Q_x&=6 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThey agree everywhere. The components have continuous partial derivatives on the simply connected domain $\\mathbb R^2$, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. For a conservative field with potential $f$, the fundamental theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=f(B)-f(A).\n$$\n\nFor such a conservative field, a closed path has the same endpoints, so its circulation is zero in either orientation. An open path can also have zero work when its endpoint potential values agree; zero work is not guaranteed for every open path. For example, the work along the segment from $(0, 0)$ to $(1, 0)$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, which is nonzero. A path followed by its reverse always has zero total work, so a claim of nonzero work for every closed path is false.\n\n- $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$ for every closed path $C$. This is **true**.\n- $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. This is **true**.\n- $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\ne0$ for some open path $C$. This is **true**.\n- None of the above This is **false**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709256 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- x e^{y} - 3\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(- \\frac{x^{2} e^{y}}{2} + y\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\non $\\mathbb R^2$. Which statements are true? Select all that apply. All paths mentioned are piecewise smooth and lie in $\\mathbb R^2$." :question/worked-solution "Write $P=- x e^{y} - 3$ and $Q=- \\frac{x^{2} e^{y}}{2} + y$. The cross partials are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=- x e^{y}\\\\Q_x&=- x e^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThey agree everywhere. The components have continuous partial derivatives on the simply connected domain $\\mathbb R^2$, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. For a conservative field with potential $f$, the fundamental theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=f(B)-f(A).\n$$\n\nFor such a conservative field, a closed path has the same endpoints, so its circulation is zero in either orientation. An open path can also have zero work when its endpoint potential values agree; zero work is not guaranteed for every open path. For example, the work along the segment from $(0, 0)$ to $(1, 0)$ is $- \\frac{7}{2}$, which is nonzero. A path followed by its reverse always has zero total work, so a claim of nonzero work for every closed path is false.\n\n- $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\ne0$ for some closed path $C$. This is **false**.\n- $\\mathbf F$ is **not** conservative. This is **false**.\n- $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\ne0$ for some open path $C$. This is **true**.\n- None of the above This is **false**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709259 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(6 x \\arctan{\\left(2 y \\right)}\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{6 x^{2}}{4 y^{2} + 1} - y\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\non $\\mathbb R^2$. Which statements are true? Select all that apply. All paths mentioned are piecewise smooth and lie in $\\mathbb R^2$." :question/worked-solution "Write $P=6 x \\arctan{\\left(2 y \\right)}$ and $Q=\\frac{6 x^{2}}{4 y^{2} + 1} - y$. The cross partials are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=\\frac{12 x}{4 y^{2} + 1}\\\\Q_x&=\\frac{12 x}{4 y^{2} + 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThey agree everywhere. The components have continuous partial derivatives on the simply connected domain $\\mathbb R^2$, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. For a conservative field with potential $f$, the fundamental theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=f(B)-f(A).\n$$\n\nFor such a conservative field, a closed path has the same endpoints, so its circulation is zero in either orientation. An open path can also have zero work when its endpoint potential values agree; zero work is not guaranteed for every open path. For example, the work along the segment from $(0, 0)$ to $(0, 1)$ is $- \\frac{1}{2}$, which is nonzero. A path followed by its reverse always has zero total work, so a claim of nonzero work for every closed path is false.\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. This is **true**.\n- $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\ne0$ for some open path $C$. This is **true**.\n- $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\ne0$ for some closed path $C$. This is **false**.\n- None of the above This is **false**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709264 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(\\frac{2 x}{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(\\frac{2 y}{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\non $\\mathbb R^2$. Which statements are true? Select all that apply. All paths mentioned are piecewise smooth and lie in $\\mathbb R^2$." :question/worked-solution "Write $P=\\frac{2 x}{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}$ and $Q=\\frac{2 y}{x^{2} + y^{2} + 1}$. The cross partials are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=- \\frac{4 x y}{\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} + 1\\right)^{2}}\\\\Q_x&=- \\frac{4 x y}{\\left(x^{2} + y^{2} + 1\\right)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThey agree everywhere. The components have continuous partial derivatives on the simply connected domain $\\mathbb R^2$, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. For a conservative field with potential $f$, the fundamental theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=f(B)-f(A).\n$$\n\nFor such a conservative field, a closed path has the same endpoints, so its circulation is zero in either orientation. An open path can also have zero work when its endpoint potential values agree; zero work is not guaranteed for every open path. For example, the work along the segment from $(0, 0)$ to $(1, 0)$ is $\\log{\\left(2 \\right)}$, which is nonzero. A path followed by its reverse always has zero total work, so a claim of nonzero work for every closed path is false.\n\n- $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\ne0$ for every closed path $C$. This is **false**.\n- $\\mathbf F$ is **not** conservative. This is **false**.\n- $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\ne0$ for some open path $C$. This is **true**.\n- None of the above This is **false**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709294 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- x^{2} + 2 y\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(3 x - 1\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\non $\\mathbb R^2$. Which statements are true? Select all that apply. All paths mentioned are piecewise smooth and lie in $\\mathbb R^2$." :question/worked-solution "Write $P=- x^{2} + 2 y$ and $Q=3 x - 1$. The cross partials are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=2\\\\Q_x&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThey do not agree identically. Equality of the cross partials is necessary for a smooth field to be conservative, so $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative. On this domain, if all closed-path integrals were zero, path independence would give a potential. Thus some closed path has nonzero circulation. For a conservative field with potential $f$, the fundamental theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=f(B)-f(A).\n$$\n\nFor such a conservative field, a closed path has the same endpoints, so its circulation is zero in either orientation. An open path can also have zero work when its endpoint potential values agree; zero work is not guaranteed for every open path. For example, the work along the segment from $(0, 0)$ to $(1, 0)$ is $- \\frac{1}{3}$, which is nonzero. A path followed by its reverse always has zero total work, so a claim of nonzero work for every closed path is false.\n\n- $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\ne0$ for some open path $C$. This is **true**.\n- $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0$ for every closed path $C$. This is **false**.\n- $\\mathbf F$ is **not** conservative. This is **true**.\n- None of the above This is **false**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709367 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(- y - 1\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(- x + 4 y\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\non $\\mathbb R^2$. The diagram shows three piecewise-smooth directed paths: $\\alpha$ goes from $A$ to $B$, $\\gamma$ goes from $C$ to $B$, $\\beta$ goes from $C$ to $A$.\n\n![alpha from A to B; gamma from C to B; beta from C to A](images/a1/a158314bab6964f974612675168ca0ae56cd9cbe57b24d1e692182f5d3b4db50.png)\n\nFor a directed path $\\eta$, write $W_\\eta=\\int_\\eta\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$. You are given $W_{\\alpha}=-1$ and $W_{\\gamma}=-1$. Which statements are true? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Write $P=- y - 1$ and $Q=- x + 4 y$. The cross partials are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=-1\\\\Q_x&=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThey agree everywhere. The components have continuous partial derivatives on the simply connected domain $\\mathbb R^2$, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. For a conservative field with potential $f$, the fundamental theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=f(B)-f(A).\n$$\n\nFor such a conservative field, a closed path has the same endpoints, so its circulation is zero in either orientation. An open path can also have zero work when its endpoint potential values agree; zero work is not guaranteed for every open path. Follow the route $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$. Relative to the displayed arrows, its edge signs give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W_{\\alpha}-W_{\\gamma}+W_{\\beta}&=0\\\\W_{\\beta}&=0\\\\W_{\\beta}&=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReversing an edge changes its work sign. The proposed cycle sum evaluates to $2$. Compare the proposed missing work with $0$.\n\n- $W_{\\beta}=0$. This is **true**.\n- $\\oint_L\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\ne0$ for some closed path $L$ in $\\mathbb R^2$. This is **false**.\n- $\\mathbf F$ is **not** conservative. This is **false**.\n- $-W_{\\alpha}-W_{\\gamma}+W_{\\beta}=0$. This is **false**.\n- None of the above This is **false**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709373 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(2 - y\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(- x - 6 y - 1\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\non $\\mathbb R^2$. The diagram shows three piecewise-smooth directed paths: $\\gamma$ goes from $B$ to $A$, $\\alpha$ goes from $C$ to $B$, $\\beta$ goes from $C$ to $A$.\n\n![gamma from B to A; alpha from C to B; beta from C to A](images/9a/9aee246533363494c0bc46bee33d742c79ed014a3c23599d5a5ebfd621801457.png)\n\nFor a directed path $\\eta$, write $W_\\eta=\\int_\\eta\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$. You are given $W_{\\alpha}=5$ and $W_{\\beta}=3$. Which statements are true? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Write $P=2 - y$ and $Q=- x - 6 y - 1$. The cross partials are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=-1\\\\Q_x&=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThey agree everywhere. The components have continuous partial derivatives on the simply connected domain $\\mathbb R^2$, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. For a conservative field with potential $f$, the fundamental theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=f(B)-f(A).\n$$\n\nFor such a conservative field, a closed path has the same endpoints, so its circulation is zero in either orientation. An open path can also have zero work when its endpoint potential values agree; zero work is not guaranteed for every open path. Follow the route $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$. Relative to the displayed arrows, its edge signs give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-W_{\\gamma}-W_{\\alpha}+W_{\\beta}&=0\\\\-W_{\\gamma}&=2\\\\W_{\\gamma}&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReversing an edge changes its work sign. The proposed cycle sum evaluates to $10$. Compare the proposed missing work with $-2$.\n\n- $-W_{\\gamma}+W_{\\alpha}+W_{\\beta}=0$. This is **false**.\n- $\\oint_L\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\ne0$ for some closed path $L$ in $\\mathbb R^2$. This is **false**.\n- $\\mathbf F$ is **not** conservative. This is **false**.\n- $W_{\\gamma}=0$. This is **false**.\n- None of the above This is **true**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186709378 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\left(3 y + 2\\right)\\mathbf i+\\left(3 x + 4 y - 1\\right)\\mathbf j\n$$\n\non $\\mathbb R^2$. The diagram shows three piecewise-smooth directed paths: $\\beta$ goes from $A$ to $B$, $\\alpha$ goes from $C$ to $B$, $\\gamma$ goes from $C$ to $A$.\n\n![beta from A to B; alpha from C to B; gamma from C to A](images/ca/ca9a13effdc9d90412cd0504a55ccbe4765c726ba2846d254abdcbd0d9a9628d.png)\n\nFor a directed path $\\eta$, write $W_\\eta=\\int_\\eta\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$. You are given $W_{\\alpha}=-4$ and $W_{\\gamma}=-6$. Which statements are true? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Write $P=3 y + 2$ and $Q=3 x + 4 y - 1$. The cross partials are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=3\\\\Q_x&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThey agree everywhere. The components have continuous partial derivatives on the simply connected domain $\\mathbb R^2$, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative. For a conservative field with potential $f$, the fundamental theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=f(B)-f(A).\n$$\n\nFor such a conservative field, a closed path has the same endpoints, so its circulation is zero in either orientation. An open path can also have zero work when its endpoint potential values agree; zero work is not guaranteed for every open path. Follow the route $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$. Relative to the displayed arrows, its edge signs give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W_{\\beta}-W_{\\alpha}+W_{\\gamma}&=0\\\\W_{\\beta}&=2\\\\W_{\\beta}&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReversing an edge changes its work sign. The proposed cycle sum evaluates to $0$. Compare the proposed missing work with $2$.\n\n- $\\oint_L\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\ne0$ for some closed path $L$ in $\\mathbb R^2$. This is **false**.\n- $\\mathbf F$ is **not** conservative. This is **false**.\n- $W_{\\beta}=2$. This is **true**.\n- $W_{\\beta}-W_{\\alpha}+W_{\\gamma}=0$. This is **true**.\n- None of the above This is **false**."}]}
